-- Q731: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Adding capital assets and removing related expenditures","Removing all long-term liabilities from the fund balance","Converting the basis from accrual to modified accrual","Eliminating all intergovernmental transfer activity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 77, the fund-to-government-wide reconciliation converts from modified accrual to full accrual. Capital assets not reported in fund statements are added back, and related capital expenditures are removed. This bridges the current financial resources and economic resources measurement focuses.
Wrong (B): Long-term liabilities are added (not removed) in the reconciliation because fund statements exclude them and government-wide statements include them.
Wrong (C): The conversion goes from modified accrual to full accrual, not the reverse direction.
Wrong (D): Intergovernmental transfers between governmental funds are eliminated at the government-wide level, but this is not the primary reconciling adjustment for the balance sheet.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 731;

-- Q732: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They use full accrual accounting unlike other governmental funds","The principal must be preserved and only earnings may be spent","They are classified as proprietary funds under GASB standards","They are restricted exclusively to debt service obligations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB standards, permanent funds hold resources where the principal must remain intact, functioning like an endowment. Only earnings on the invested principal may be used for the specified governmental purpose, such as park maintenance in this case.
Wrong (A): Permanent funds use modified accrual accounting like all other governmental funds, not full accrual.
Wrong (C): Permanent funds are classified as governmental funds, not proprietary funds.
Wrong (D): Debt service funds, not permanent funds, are used for principal and interest payments on long-term debt.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 732;

-- Q734: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Avoid recording infrastructure assets on the financial statements entirely","Expense maintenance costs instead of depreciation if condition levels are met","Depreciate all infrastructure assets uniformly over a 50-year useful life","Report infrastructure assets at fair value at each reporting date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 23, the modified approach allows governments to expense preservation costs instead of recording depreciation for eligible infrastructure assets. The government must maintain an asset management system, perform condition assessments at least every three years, and demonstrate assets are maintained at or above an established condition level.
Wrong (A): Infrastructure assets must still be recorded and reported; the modified approach only changes the depreciation treatment.
Wrong (C): GASB 34 does not prescribe a uniform 50-year life; the modified approach eliminates depreciation entirely when conditions are met.
Wrong (D): Infrastructure assets are reported at historical cost, not fair value, under GASB standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 734;

-- Q736: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
