  choices = $EXPL$["Fund statements report the premium as an other financing source increasing fund balance, while government-wide statements include it in the total bond liability amortized over the bond''s life","Both statements report the premium as revenue since it represents cash received in excess of the bond''s face value","Fund statements report the premium as a long-term liability while government-wide statements expense it immediately in the period of issuance","Both statements amortize the premium identically over the bond''s life because GASB 34 requires uniform treatment at all reporting levels"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the current financial resources measurement focus, governmental fund statements recognize the $400,000 premium as an other financing source that increases fund balance in the current period. Under the economic resources measurement focus (GASB 34), the government-wide statements report the full $10.4 million as a long-term liability, with the premium amortized as a reduction of interest expense over the bond''s life.
Wrong (B): Bond premiums are classified as other financing sources in governmental accounting, not revenue.
Wrong (C): This reverses the treatment — fund statements do not report long-term liabilities; government-wide statements do.
Wrong (D): GASB 34 intentionally requires different treatments at the two reporting levels due to different measurement focuses.$EXPL$
