-- Q737: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All governmental funds regardless of whether they have adopted a budget","The general fund and each major special revenue fund with a legally adopted budget","Enterprise funds and internal service funds that receive appropriations","All funds that receive federal grant funding requiring compliance reporting"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): GASB requires budgetary comparison schedules as RSI for the general fund and each major special revenue fund that has a legally adopted annual budget. These schedules compare original budget, final amended budget, and actual results on the budgetary basis.
Wrong (A): Only major funds with legally adopted budgets require these schedules, not all governmental funds regardless of budget status.
Wrong (C): Enterprise and internal service funds are proprietary funds and are not subject to the budgetary comparison RSI requirement.
Wrong (D): Federal grant funding does not determine the RSI requirement; the criteria are major fund status and having a legally adopted annual budget.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 737;

-- Q738: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both are reported identically in both statements because GASB 34 harmonized the measurement focuses for all transactions","Both are expenditures in fund statements that reduce fund balance; in government-wide statements the vehicle is capitalized and the debt payment reduces the liability","The vehicle is capitalized at the fund level and the debt payment is expensed; government-wide statements expense both as period costs","Both reduce cash equally in both statements and the measurement focus only affects classification labels, not reported amounts"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the current financial resources measurement focus, governmental fund statements treat both the $200,000 vehicle purchase and $150,000 debt payment as expenditures totaling $350,000 that reduce fund balance. Under the economic resources measurement focus (GASB 34), the vehicle is capitalized as a $200,000 asset and depreciated, while the debt payment reduces the bond liability by $150,000 with no expense recorded.
Wrong (A): GASB 34 intentionally maintains different measurement focuses, producing different reporting for these transactions.
Wrong (C): This reverses the treatment — fund statements expense both items; government-wide statements capitalize the vehicle.
Wrong (D): The measurement focus changes both the amounts and classifications, not just labels.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 738;

-- Q740: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The tax return is filed with the collecting jurisdiction","The underlying exchange transaction occurs and resources are available","The government formally budgets the expected revenue amount","Cash is physically received by the government treasury"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 33, paragraph 16, derived tax revenues such as sales taxes are recognized when the underlying exchange transaction occurs and the resources are available. For sales taxes, recognition occurs when the retail sale takes place, provided collections are received within the availability period (typically 60 days after fiscal year-end).
Wrong (A): Filing the tax return is an administrative event, not the recognition trigger for derived tax revenues.
Wrong (C): Budgeting revenue does not create a recognition event; GASB 33 ties recognition to the underlying taxable transaction.
Wrong (D): Cash-basis recognition is not permitted under modified accrual; the availability criterion allows accrual of amounts expected to be collected within the availability period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 740;

-- Q1066: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both stages are capitalized because all costs necessary for the project qualify as capitalizable assets under GAAP","Preliminary stage costs are expensed and application development costs are capitalized based on project certainty and commitment level","Both stages are expensed because internal-use software costs are never capitalizable under GAAP standards","Vendor evaluation costs are capitalized while coding costs are expensed because evaluation requires more specialized expertise"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 350-40 defines three stages for internal-use software: preliminary project, application development, and post-implementation. Preliminary stage costs ($80,000 for vendor evaluation) are expensed because the outcome is uncertain. Application development costs ($350,000 for coding and testing) are capitalized once management commits to the project and future economic benefit is probable.
Wrong (A): ASC 350-40 uses a stage-based framework; preliminary stage costs are always expensed regardless of project necessity.
Wrong (C): ASC 350-40 explicitly permits capitalization of application development stage costs for internal-use software.
Wrong (D): This reverses the treatment — vendor evaluation is a preliminary stage cost that must be expensed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1066;

-- Q1070: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both arrangements are treated identically because cloud computing costs are always expensed regardless of classification","CCA-1 records a software asset while CCA-2 records an operating expense because hosted arrangements provide more control","Neither arrangement creates a balance sheet asset because all cloud computing costs are period expenses under GAAP","CCA-1 capitalizes implementation costs as a prepaid asset while CCA-2 records a software intangible asset under ASC 350-40"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASU 2018-15, the accounting depends on whether the customer obtains control of the software. CCA-2 (software license) gives Glendale the ability to take possession, so $500,000 is capitalized as an intangible asset under ASC 350-40. CCA-1 (service contract) capitalizes qualifying implementation costs as a prepaid asset amortized over the service term. Cash flows are operating for CCA-1 and investing for CCA-2.
Wrong (A): Both ASC 350-40 and ASU 2018-15 provide capitalization provisions for qualifying costs in cloud arrangements.
Wrong (B): This reverses the classifications — service contracts create prepaid assets, not software assets.
Wrong (C): Both arrangements can create balance sheet assets, though the nature of the asset differs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1070;

-- Q1071: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All $120,000 is capitalized because any cost related to a cloud implementation qualifies as an implementation cost under ASU 2018-15","The $50,000 coding qualifies for capitalization as application development activity; data migration and training are expensed","Only the $25,000 training cost is capitalizable because employee readiness is the most valuable SaaS investment","All $120,000 must be expensed because SaaS service contracts prohibit any form of cost capitalization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): ASU 2018-15 applies the same ASC 350-40 stage framework to SaaS implementation costs. The $50,000 custom interface coding is application development stage activity and qualifies for capitalization as a prepaid asset. Data migration ($45,000) is a preliminary or data conversion activity that is expensed. Training ($25,000) is a post-implementation cost explicitly excluded from capitalization.
Wrong (A): The stage-based framework distinguishes qualifying costs; not all implementation spending is capitalizable.
Wrong (C): Training is explicitly excluded from capitalization under both ASC 350-40 and ASU 2018-15.
Wrong (D): ASU 2018-15 specifically permits capitalization of qualifying implementation costs for service contract arrangements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1071;

-- Q1072: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Prior years'' amortization is restated to reflect the 3-year life, increasing accumulated amortization for each prior year","The company must switch from straight-line to accelerated amortization to catch up on the previously underreported expense","The remaining $360,000 book value is amortized over the remaining 1 year as a prospective change in accounting estimate","No adjustment is needed because the original 5-year useful life estimate is locked in at the capitalization date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 250, a change in useful life is a change in accounting estimate handled prospectively. The remaining book value ($600,000 minus $240,000 = $360,000) is amortized over the remaining useful life (3 years total minus 2 years elapsed = 1 year), producing $360,000 of amortization in Year 3. This is not a change in accounting principle because only the period estimate changed.
Wrong (A): Retrospective restatement applies to changes in accounting principle, not changes in estimate.
Wrong (B): A life revision does not require changing the amortization method; straight-line continues with the new remaining period.
Wrong (D): ASC 250 explicitly requires revision of useful life estimates when new information emerges.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1072;

-- Q1076: Advanced Lease Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both scenarios qualify as sales because legal title transfer is the sole criterion for sale recognition under ASC 842","Scenario A qualifies as a sale because a fair-value repurchase option does not prevent control transfer; Scenario B fails as a sale because the below-market option creates a significant economic incentive to reacquire","Scenario A fails as a sale but Scenario B qualifies, because below-market options are treated as purchase commitments transferring control","Neither scenario qualifies as a sale because any repurchase option disqualifies the transfer regardless of its terms"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-40-25-3, sale-leaseback transfers are evaluated under ASC 606''s control criteria. A fair-value repurchase option (Scenario A) does not prevent sale recognition because the buyer-lessor bears residual value risk. A below-market repurchase option (Scenario B) creates a significant economic incentive to reacquire, so the buyer-lessor does not obtain control and the transaction is accounted for as financing.
Wrong (A): Legal title alone does not determine sale recognition; economic substance and control transfer govern under ASC 606.
Wrong (C): This reverses the analysis — fair-value options permit sales while below-market options prevent them.
Wrong (D): Fair-value repurchase options do not disqualify a transfer from sale treatment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1076;

-- Q1078: Advanced Lease Transactions [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both modifications are treated as new separate contracts because any lease change constitutes a new agreement under ASC 842","Scenario A is a separate contract because it adds scope at a commensurate price; Scenario B requires remeasurement because extending the term without new scope does not qualify","Scenario A requires remeasurement while Scenario B is a separate contract, because term extensions always create separate contracts","Neither modification affects the ROU asset because lease modifications only impact the lease liability balance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-10-25-8, a lease modification is treated as a separate contract if it grants additional right of use (new scope) and the consideration increase is commensurate with standalone price. Scenario A meets both criteria, so the existing lease continues unchanged and a new ROU asset is recognized. Scenario B only extends the term at below-market rates, so it requires remeasurement of the existing lease liability and ROU asset.
Wrong (A): ASC 842 distinguishes between modifications qualifying as separate contracts and those requiring remeasurement.
Wrong (C): This reverses the treatment — term extensions without new scope require remeasurement, not separate contract treatment.
Wrong (D): Remeasurement adjusts both the lease liability and the ROU asset, not just the liability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1078;

-- Q1084: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$200,000 gain recognized on the excess of sale price over carrying value","$0 because the fixed-price repurchase option causes a failed sale treated as financing","$100,000 gain recognized as a partial sale with proportional allocation","$600,000 gain recognized on the full sale price received in cash"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-40-25-3 and ASC 606, a fixed-price repurchase option means the buyer-lessor does not obtain control of the asset. The transaction fails to qualify as a sale, so no gain is recognized. The seller-lessee keeps the asset on its books and records the $600,000 cash received as a financing obligation.
Wrong (A): No gain can be recognized because the transaction does not qualify as a sale under the control transfer criteria.
Wrong (C): Partial sale treatment does not apply when the entire transaction fails the control transfer test.
