-- Q1085: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Make no adjustment until the renewal option is formally exercised by the lessee","Remeasure the lease liability for the revised 7-year term at the current discount rate and adjust the ROU asset","Record a new separate lease for the 3-year renewal period as a distinct right-of-use asset","Adjust only future ROU asset amortization prospectively without remeasuring the lease liability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-10-35-1, when a significant event makes it reasonably certain a renewal option will be exercised, the lessee must remeasure the lease liability using the revised remaining term (4 + 3 = 7 years) at a revised discount rate. The ROU asset is adjusted by the same amount as the liability change. This reassessment uses the rate at the reassessment date.
Wrong (A): ASC 842 requires immediate remeasurement when reasonable certainty changes, not deferral until formal exercise.
Wrong (C): A reassessment of the existing lease term does not create a separate lease; it modifies the existing measurement.
Wrong (D): Both the lease liability and ROU asset must be remeasured, not just the amortization pattern.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1085;

-- Q1086: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The $500,000 loss has no balance sheet effect because actuarial gains and losses are disclosed only in footnotes","The funded status worsens to a $2,000,000 liability and the actuarial loss is recognized in OCI as a smoothing mechanism","The $500,000 is immediately recognized as pension expense, increasing the income statement charge in the current period","The actuarial loss reduces plan assets but does not change the funded status because gains and losses are netted to zero"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715, the funded status (PBO minus plan assets) is reported on the balance sheet. The $500,000 actuarial loss reduces plan assets from $10,500,000 to $10,000,000, increasing the liability from $1,500,000 to $2,000,000. The loss is recognized in OCI rather than pension expense because actuarial estimates fluctuate, and OCI smooths income statement volatility via the corridor approach.
Wrong (A): The funded status on the balance sheet changes immediately when actuarial gains or losses occur.
Wrong (C): ASC 715 explicitly avoids immediate income statement recognition for actuarial items, using OCI instead.
Wrong (D): The funded status changes by the full $500,000; actuarial losses are not netted to zero.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1086;

-- Q1088: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both plans produce identical accounting because GAAP does not distinguish between defined contribution and defined benefit plans","Defined contribution expense equals the contribution with no balance sheet liability; defined benefit requires actuarial estimates and funded status reporting","Defined contribution plans are more complex because employers must track individual participant accounts and investment returns","Defined benefit plans have simpler accounting because the fixed benefit formula eliminates all estimation uncertainty"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715, a defined contribution plan''s expense equals the employer''s contribution each period, with no further liability once the contribution is made. A defined benefit plan requires estimating the PBO using actuarial assumptions (discount rate, salary growth, mortality), reporting the funded status as a balance sheet liability, and calculating multi-component pension expense including service cost, interest cost, and expected return.
Wrong (A): ASC 715 establishes fundamentally different accounting for these two plan types based on who bears the risk.
Wrong (C): Individual account tracking is an administrative function, not the source of accounting complexity.
Wrong (D): The benefit promise in defined benefit plans creates substantial estimation uncertainty, not simplicity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1088;

-- Q1089: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The discount rate decrease reduces the PBO because lower rates produce smaller nominal future obligation amounts","The discount rate change has no PBO effect because the PBO depends on salary projections rather than interest rates","The $2,800,000 increase is immediately recognized as pension expense, reducing net income in the current period","The lower discount rate increases the PBO by $2,800,000 because present value of fixed future payments rises at lower rates"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 715, the PBO is the present value of estimated future benefit payments discounted at high-quality corporate bond yields. When the discount rate decreases from 5% to 4%, the same future payment stream has a higher present value — this is the fundamental inverse relationship. The $2,800,000 increase is an actuarial loss recognized in OCI, increasing the funded status liability on the balance sheet.
Wrong (A): This reverses the relationship — lower discount rates increase present values and thus increase the PBO.
Wrong (B): The discount rate is a primary driver of PBO magnitude; salary projections are only one component.
Wrong (C): ASC 715 avoids immediate income statement recognition for actuarial items; the loss goes to OCI.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1089;

-- Q1093: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The $3,600,000 is recognized as pension expense immediately because the benefit increase is effective in the amendment year","The amendment has no financial statement effect because retroactive benefit increases are disclosed only in footnotes","The $3,600,000 is recognized in OCI at amendment and amortized into pension expense at $300,000 per year over 12 years","The $3,600,000 reduces plan assets because the amendment requires immediate funding of the full retroactive amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 715, a plan amendment granting retroactive benefit increases creates prior service cost equal to the PBO increase ($3,600,000). This amount is recognized in OCI at the amendment date and amortized into pension expense over the 12-year average remaining service period ($300,000/year). OCI-first treatment matches the cost to the periods of expected future employee service.
Wrong (A): Immediate expense recognition violates the matching principle; the cost relates to 12 years of future service.
Wrong (B): Plan amendments create real financial statement recognition, not just footnote disclosure.
Wrong (D): The amendment changes the PBO, not plan assets; accounting recognition and funding are separate obligations.$EXPL$
